{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785445023,"nonce":"VgJ3WnnIBxPnjl+Z9T/epZWYCW4=","message":"Recap of the request and thread discussion:\n\nProblem: OpenEmbedded BSPs carry patches with an \"Upstream-Status\"\ntrailer (a Yocto QA convention for tracking whether a patch has been\nsent/accepted upstream). When those patches are mainlined, the trailer\nmust currently be stripped by hand or with ad-hoc scripts, because it\nis meaningless (and noisy) on a kernel mailing list.\n\nRequest: teach b4 to drop a configurable list of trailers at send time.\n\"Upstream-Status\" is the motivating example, but the ask is explicitly\nfor a general mechanism, not a hardcoded single trailer.\n\nThread consensus and refinements:\n- A generalizable filter also covers Gerrit-style \"Change-Id\" trailers\n  (Mark Brown); u-boot's patman has long had exactly such a Change-Id\n  filter (Chen-Yu Tsai), so there is prior art for the UX.\n- Scope is send-time filtering only. Ted Ts'o suggested a git hook on\n  the applying side, but Alexandre Belloni clarified the request is\n  about filtering when *sending*, not applying — the trailers should\n  remain in the maintainer's local/BSP tree and only be omitted from\n  the outgoing series.\n- Konrad Dybcio suggested the filtering should be loud, e.g. a warning\n  from `b4 prep --check`, so senders are aware trailers are\n  being dropped.\n- Michael S. Tsirkin noted a general trailer-removal list could also be\n  used to suppress message-id trailers for those who apply with them.\n\nImplementation sketch: a config knob (e.g. b4.send-trailer-filter or\nper-project equivalent) holding a list of trailer names to strip from\ncommit messages when generating outgoing mail, applied in the prep/send\npath with a visible notice in `b4 prep --check` output. Local commits\nare never rewritten.","files":null}]}